WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:11.920
It has been estimated that as much as 95&nbsp;
percent of the world’s data will never be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:18.000
seen by human eyes. Much of this unseen data&nbsp;
is called M to M data or machine to machine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.600
There are many sources of machine generated data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:27.040
Examples include, when the cell phones ping&nbsp;
to the cell towers to check where they are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:32.960
when the satellite radio and the GPS connect&nbsp;
to locate the car or your phone, the RFID or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:37.920
radio frequency identification tag readings&nbsp;
on billions of small objects, readings from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:46.160
medical devices and web crawlers and spam bots.
Perhaps the most interesting part of the machine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:52.160
to machine communications falls under the rubric&nbsp;
of the Internet of Things. It is estimated that by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:57.840
2025, as many as 30 billion uniquely identifiable&nbsp;
devices may be connected to the internet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:04.720
This actually requires a major change in how&nbsp;
the internet works. Basically, everything will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:08.800
have a chip and it will be connected to the&nbsp;
internet and they’ll be talking to each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:14.960
sharing information. So when you hear people talk&nbsp;
about smart sensors, in your home or in your city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:19.520
or on your air conditioning or the smart home,&nbsp;
which knows when to turn lights on or change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.320
the temperature, or the smart grids where the&nbsp;
city generates and sends out the electricity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:28.880
or the smart city itself which coordinates all&nbsp;
of the traffic and the utilities as a way of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:33.920
being more efficient and more economical in&nbsp;
providing better service. All of this would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:39.840
be enabled by small objects communicating&nbsp;
one with another in the internet of things.&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:46.160
Some of the uses for this can include putting&nbsp;
sensors on production lines to monitor systems for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:51.120
when they need maintenance, or the smart meters&nbsp;
on utility systems to shut them off at peak times.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:57.040
Also things like infrastructure management, that&nbsp;
talks about control and monitoring of bridges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:04.240
railways, wind farms, traffic systems. Industrial&nbsp;
applications like manufacturing process controls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:08.720
supply chain network, having predictive&nbsp;
maintenance or integration with a smart grid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:14.400
to optimize energy consumption. There is energy&nbsp;
management, that’s the switches and outlets and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:19.760
bulbs and TVs and screens and heating systems,&nbsp;
controlling ovens, changing lighting conditions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:24.000
And then, medical and healthcare systems.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.480
Basically, anything that’s mechanical can be&nbsp;
eventually hooked up and communicating through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:34.240
the internet of things. All of which generates&nbsp;
an enormous amount of data as they talk one to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:39.840
another and coordinate their own activities. So,&nbsp;
in looking at the differences between machine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:44.720
generated data and human generated data, the&nbsp;
most obvious difference is that all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:50.560
machine generated data is machine readable. It&nbsp;
can be immediately searched and read and mined.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:55.760
It’s high on volume and velocity, two&nbsp;
of the characteristics of big data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:03.840
but low on variety, which makes it&nbsp;
easier for machines to deal with it.

